# Authenticated per-target source files for src\xhci98.inf's usbd.sys copy.
#
# usbhub20.sys imports USBD.SYS on both targets, and nothing on an xHCI-only
# machine ever places that file (docs\contributing\lessons.md, "usbhub20.sys bugchecks Win2000"). xhci98.inf
# therefore carries it - but "it" is two different binaries with the same
# destination name, so the media keeps them apart under distinct source names
# and each install path names exactly one of them.
#
# That split is only as good as what is actually staged under each media name.
# A swap here would install Win98 SE's 4.10.2222 usbd.sys on Windows 2000 and
# is invisible afterwards: both files are called usbd.sys on the target, and
# the wrong one fails at usbhub20.sys's next load rather than at install time.
# So every row carries the file's identity, and both
# scripts\package\make-package.ps1 (before staging) and
# scripts\inf-gate\check-inf.ps1 -PackageDir (after staging) check it. This is
# the same rule the import gate's evidence manifests learned: a hash recorded
# in prose does nothing unless something compares it.
#
# Both files are the target OS's own build, taken from that OS's install
# media by scripts\package\extract-usbd-sources.ps1 - nothing foreign is
# introduced onto either system, and neither file is tracked by this repository
# (tools\ is git-ignored).
#
# They GO in the GitHub release download, by a decision recorded
# in docs\contributing\legal-provenance.md section 5. Read that in the tense
# section 5 uses: it is a decided channel, and so far nothing has
# actually been uploaded - the repository is still private and no release has
# been published. What it changes today is that the hashes below are
# load-bearing in one more place than they used to be: make-release.ps1
# re-checks them against the assembled upload tree, because a swapped pair is
# invisible after install and that asset is what a user would install from.
#
# Format, per row:
#   MEDIA-NAME  TARGET  REPO\RELATIVE\SOURCE  VERSION  LENGTH  SHA256  note
#
# MEDIA-NAME is the name the file has in the staged package and in the INF's
# [SourceDisksFiles]; TARGET is win98 or win2000 and must match the install
# path that names it.
#
# **A target may name more than one file.** It could not until batch 13-E, when
# batch 13-E added Windows 98's composite parent usbhub.sys - a second file that
# an xHCI-only Windows 98 machine never gets from setup, for exactly the reason
# usbd.sys does not: Win98 ships USB.INF on every install but copies the USB
# driver *files* only when it detects a controller. usbhub.sys is win98 ONLY:
# on Windows 2000 that name belongs to the OS's own USB 1.1 hub driver and the
# composite parent is usbccgp.sys, which this package does not carry. The INF
# gate enforces that asymmetry in both directions (W98-MISSING, W98-ONWIN2K).

usbd98.sys  win98    tools\win98se-extracted\usbd.sys   4.10.2222      18912 0118DB141D3FD7A51C72AB71D575939794FE5985CAF76D7570A352C00AF6F56A Windows 98 SE's own usbd.sys, BASE5.CAB (layout.inf: "usbd.sys=5,,18912")
usbd2k.sys  win2000  tools\win2ksp4-extracted\USBD.SYS  5.00.2195.6658 20688 45EE7C3D552E31BD21512E7C1D0190512492A8ADC9EE67B4A84D4A5D8EEB8927 Windows 2000 SP4's own usbd.sys, I386\USBD.SY_ on the retail SP4 ISO
usbhub98.sys win98   tools\win98se-extracted\usbhub.sys 4.10.2222      35680 E898B75F2449EB9E5BBCB3FADF7387C9819F1C7D2C6D49BAD83D8236F46AFC31 Windows 98 SE's own usbhub.sys - the COMPOSITE PARENT. Win98-only: on Win2000 that name is the OS's own hub driver and the composite parent is usbccgp.sys
